{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgheAyhhMbeNqYZGsSf5c","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Dq ≈ 890 cm⁻¹ and B ≈ 790 cm⁻¹**, from a fit to ν₁ and ν₃ only; the predicted ν₂ is about 900 cm⁻¹ below the observed band.\n\n**Assignment.** The complex is a high-spin d⁷ ion such as Co(II). The three bands are assigned as follows:\n- ν₁ = ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴T₂g(F), at 8 620 cm⁻¹\n- ν₂ = ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴A₂g(F), at 18 410 cm⁻¹\n- ν₃ = ⁴T₁g(F) → ⁴T₁g(P), at 20 250 cm⁻¹\n\n**Model.** Energies are measured from the ⁴F term barycentre. The ⁴T₁g(F) and ⁴T₁g(P) states mix, with diagonal elements −6Dq and 15B and an off-diagonal element of magnitude 6Dq. The other two states are ⁴T₂g at 2Dq and ⁴A₂g at 12Dq.\n\nThe two ⁴T₁g eigenvalues are\n\nE± = (15B − 6Dq)/2 ± ½√[(15B + 6Dq)² + 144Dq²]\n\nThe transition energies are then:\n- ν₁ = 2Dq − E₋\n- ν₂ = 12Dq − E₋\n- ν₃ = E₊ − E₋\n\n**Fit.** I used ν₁ and ν₃, because ν₂ is a weak, two-electron-like band that is less reliable.\n\n1. The trace condition E₊ + E₋ = 15B − 6Dq, combined with ν₁ and ν₃, gives 15B = 10Dq + 3010.\n2. Substituting this into the ν₁ equation gives Dq² + 240.8·Dq − 1.0025×10⁶ = 0.\n3. The positive root is Dq ≈ 888 cm⁻¹.\n4. Then 15B ≈ 11 890 cm⁻¹, so B ≈ 793 cm⁻¹.\n\n**Check.** The fitted values predict ν₂ ≈ 17 500 cm⁻¹, against 18 410 cm⁻¹ observed. That is a modest deviation. B ≈ 790 cm⁻¹ is about 80% of the free-ion value for Co²⁺ (roughly 970 cm⁻¹), which is reasonable.\n\n{\"Dq\": 890, \"B\": 790, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"High-spin d7 Tanabe-Sugano fit to ν1 and ν3 (ν2 predicted ~17500 vs 18410 observed); C is not determinable from spin-allowed bands.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":806,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":806,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":10,"servi